zfsbackup config store -- Alembic migrations

Layout: this directory ships INSIDE the zfsbackup package
(zfsbackup/config/store/migrations/). `migrate.py` builds the Alembic Config
programmatically from this path; the repo-root alembic.ini is a developer
convenience only and is never read at runtime.

Adding a revision means editing THREE things together
-----------------------------------------------------
1. the new revision file in versions/ (alembic revision -m "...");
2. EXPECTED_HEAD in zfsbackup/config/store/migrate.py -> the new revision id;
3. KNOWN_REVISIONS in the same file -> add the new revision id (keep the old
   ones: a database may still be recorded at any of them).

Why: the daemon's startup check (migrate.check_schema) decides from those two
constants and reads no migration scripts, so a packaging problem here cannot
break daemon start. The price is that the constants must track the scripts.
migrate._assert_scripts_match_constants checks them against the scripts on
every write path (migrate, import) and raises MigrationPackagingError on any
drift, and tests/test_zfsbackup_store_upgrade.py::TestExpectedHeadConstants
fails CI if they disagree. Forgetting one of the three therefore fails loudly
in CI -- never silently at daemon start.

Rules for revision scripts
--------------------------
- Use op.batch_alter_table for anything SQLite cannot ALTER in place. Name
  every constraint; models.NAMING_CONVENTION does that for you if you let it.
- Never use autocommit_block(). The upgrade runs in ONE transaction that is
  rolled back if PRAGMA foreign_key_check finds an orphan afterwards;
  autocommit_block() commits mid-migration and defeats that rollback.
- The migration connection runs with foreign_keys=OFF (batch migrations
  rebuild tables). Rows you move or rewrite must still satisfy the foreign
  keys when you are done -- the post-migration check will refuse otherwise.
- A migration only ever runs against a database that already records an
  older, known revision. Creating a database is `zfsbackup-config import`;
  `migrate` never creates one and never touches a file without a revision.
